<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Zagwe dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1137 CE – 1270 CE (approx.) · capital Lalibela</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Agaw kings of Lasta who ruled Christian Ethiopia between Aksum and the Solomonic dynasty and carved the rock churches of Lalibela.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Kingdom of Aksum</a>, power shifted south to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agaw-people">Agaw people</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lasta">Lasta</a>. Around 1137, by the date usually given, an Agaw lord traditionally named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mara-takla-haymanot">Mara Takla Haymanot</a> took the throne, founding the Zagwe line. Later Solomonic writers treated the dynasty as usurpers, so its beginnings are poorly recorded and other chronologies place it earlier.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Zagwe kings were devout Christians who kept close ties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Church</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, which sent the head of the Ethiopian church. Their capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Roha</a>, later renamed Lalibela, became a pilgrimage centre. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebre-mesqel-lalibela">Gebre Mesqel Lalibela</a>, c. 1181 to 1221, is credited with its eleven monolithic churches cut from the rock, conceived as a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> continued through Muslim intermediaries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1270 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak">Yekuno Amlak</a>, a noble from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> claiming descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and the Aksumite kings, overthrew the last Zagwe king, founding the Solomonic line of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>. Rulers of Wag, with the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wagshum">wagshum</a>, later claimed Zagwe descent, and the churches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a> remain in use as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zagwe-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zagwe dynasty" width="320" height="321"><figcaption>Image: Taddesse Tamrat · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZagweMap.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1137 CE – 1270 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lalibela</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mara Takla Haymanot, Gebre Mesqel Lalibela, Yetbarak</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Agaw, Ge'ez</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, <a href="/countries/eritrea">Eritrea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zagwe Kingdom, Kingdom of Lasta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1204&amp;focus=zagwe-dynasty" aria-label="Zagwe dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5753 1729 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zagwe dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6073 1985L6060 1908M6073 1985L6029 1964"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6060 1908h0M6029 1964h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6073 1985h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6073 1985h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lalibela. <a href="/atlas?year=1204&amp;focus=zagwe-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
